 "verdict": "INVENTORY CONSTRAINT: fixed-point token identities were never stored. endpoint_histogram pools fixed / cyclic / wandering endpoints, and phi is the fraction of them that are fixed points -- so phi is also the histogram's PURITY. This census therefore reads only cells whose RAISED arm has phi >= 0.5, where the histogram is majority fixed-point. No model was re-run. COVERAGE: 6 readable (model, arm) pairs; 11 excluded by the purity gate or for having no gained mass; 15 absent from the files. PER PAIR, share of GAINED endpoint mass by class: llm-jp-3-1.8b [bos] word 0.69, format 0.31 (phi 0.78 -> 0.81); Falcon3-1B-Base [bos] word 0.01, format 0.99 (phi 0.21 -> 0.91); Minerva-3B-base-v1.0 [p1] word 0.00, format 1.00 (phi 0.33 -> 1.00); Falcon3-1B-Base [p1] word 0.00, format 1.00 (phi 0.21 -> 0.98); Minerva-3B-base-v1.0 [struct_t0] word 0.00, format 1.00 (phi 0.33 -> 1.00); Falcon3-1B-Base [struct_t0] word 0.00, format 1.00 (phi 0.21 -> 0.98).  PREDICTION HELD: gained mass is format-congruent (non-word) on 5 of 6 pairs, median word share 0.00. The tokens a raising prefix adds are the furniture of its own document type, not ordinary vocabulary. BOUNDARY: token class is a lexical judgement from the decoded string, fixed in the script before any output was seen. 'Gained' is per-token-id count increase from the raw arm to the raised arm, summed over census seeds. Purity is known exactly but is not perfect: at phi = 0.5 a readable histogram is still half non-fixed-point endpoints.",
 "_analysis_provenance": {
